ality  which is very much part of the culture  as well one thing that you'll hear  people talk about  is that they always have a smile on  their faces when they walk into a  patient's room  because to them that patient and  especially in this time of covid  with no family able to visit that  patient  becomes their family so this idea of  hospitality of family of caregiving  is really part and parcel of the  filipino culture  was it something you ever thought about  did you ever i'm glad you didn't but did  you ever think about it  i was supposed to there are certain  tracks that you are highly encouraged  to follow i'm the child of filipino  immigrants  and nurses doctors health care workers  engineers those are all those tracks but  i took a different  path but we're so grateful i'm glad  you're right  i know a lot of family members overseas  as well i have relatives who are doctors  and nurses who are battling covid19 each  and every day as well so  it's really truly a cultural kind of  experience  and they very much are on the front  lines  i'm so glad you're with us elaine and  the thing that stuck out to me what you  just said  about going into someone's room and  smiling and just to give a little bit of  human connection because the thing that  scares me so much about this and touches  me  is that someone is there alone and  surrounded by  no one who knows them and cares about  them  just to have somebody that smiles and  looks at you and really cares makes such  a difference  especially during these times elaine  quijano thank you so much  always good to see you
 

